in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @nl
in @mirror/data

# @mirror/data/json — JSON as a data-grammar lens.
#
# THE CANONICAL SPECIES under @mirror/data. Lifts the substrate's
# floor declaration `grammar @data/json` from
# `boot/std/data/json.mirror` (2026-05-20) into the data family at
# the mirror altitude. Two changes from the boot version:
#
#   1. `grammar @data/json` → `prism @mirror/data/json`. Per the
#      @facet/rust precedent (boot `grammar @facet/rust` → shards
#      `prism @facet/rust`): the family root altitude uses `prism`;
#      `grammar` was the pre-substrate-pull keyword for grammars
#      that hadn't yet been recognized as prisms-with-a-typed-
#      carrier. JSON IS a prism: its five-op specialization is
#      parse/emit over the json carrier.
#   2. Family placement: `@data/json` (floor) → `@mirror/data/json`
#      (mirror altitude under the @mirror/data family root). The
#      carrier and the parse/emit signatures are identical; the
#      change is naming, not shape.
#
# Per the floor declaration (boot/std/data/json.mirror):
#
#   grammar @data/json {
#     type json = null | bool(bool) | number(f64) | string(text)
#                 | array([json]) | object([(text, json)])
#     parse(text) -> json { \ }
#     emit(json) -> text { \ }
#   }
#
# The carrier exactly mirrors JSON (RFC 8259) section 3-5: six
# value kinds — null, true/false, number, string, array, object.
# Number is f64 (the JSON spec is precision-tolerant; f64 is the
# substrate's standard floating type). String is `text` (UTF-8 per
# the substrate's text type). Array is a homogeneous list of json
# values (the substrate's `[a]` list constructor). Object is an
# association list of (key, value) pairs — the substrate uses the
# pair-list form rather than a separate map type for object
# preservation order (a deliberate JSON choice per RFC 8259 §4:
# implementations MAY preserve member order).
#
# === The lens reading ===
#
# Per the family-root recognition (data-grammar IS a lens onto
# structured content): this species IS a typed surface that
# projects byte sequences through the json carrier and back.
# parse(text) -> json is the focus projection (bytes → typed
# structure); emit(json) -> text is the settle projection (typed
# structure → bytes). The substrate's running data state composes
# through this lens without dual-state divergence; round-tripping
# emit ∘ parse on canonical JSON IS structurally idempotent
# (the standard data-format-lens law, modulo number-formatting
# choices and object-key-ordering ambiguity that JSON leaves
# implementation-defined).
#
# === Substrate-pull discipline ===
#
# The carrier type and the parse/emit signatures are lifted
# verbatim. No new type; no new action; no signature change. This
# shard:
#
#   1. Names the family placement (`@mirror/data/json` is now a
#      species under `@mirror/data`).
#   2. Switches `grammar` → `prism` per the @facet/rust precedent.
#   3. Names the lens-onto-bytes reading at the family altitude.
#   4. Re-exports the json type and parse/emit actions so consumers
#      pulling `in @mirror/data/json` get the named surface.
#
# Per [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (24th-instance
# candidate; the substrate already had `@data/json` declared in
# boot/std/data/json.mirror since 2026-05-20). Per
# [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]: the same lift gave
# @cli → @mirror/lens/cli and @loss → @mirror/loss; this is the
# data-altitude instance of the same operation.
#
# === Transitional retention of boot/std/data/json.mirror ===
#
# The boot shard is RETAINED, not retired. Reasons:
#
#   - `boot/std/mcp.mirror` (line 5) imports `in @data/json` and
#     references `@data/json.parse` / `@data/json.emit` in its
#     `serve` action. Retiring the boot shard would break the
#     bootstrap; the consumer-pull migration is a separate tick.
#   - The same pattern held for `@cli` (boot/std/cli.mirror kept
#     during the lens migration; species lives at
#     shards/mirror/lens/cli.mirror) and `@loss` (boot/01a-error
#     kept during the loss family lift). Boot files retire
#     one consumer-pull at a time, not at the family-root lift.
#
# When `boot/std/mcp.mirror` migrates to `in @mirror/data/json`
# (a separate tick, likely concurrent with the mcp shard's own
# lift to `@mirror/lens/mcp` if the substrate-pull tightening
# applies), the boot json shard retires.
#
# === Family-root sibling: format-as-lens ===
#
# This species sits alongside three forward-promised siblings
# (yaml, toml, text) under @mirror/data. Per the family-root
# recognition: all four name typed lenses onto byte streams; the
# difference is the carrier shape and the format spec. json IS
# the canonical one because:
#
#   - The carrier is minimal (six variants).
#   - The format spec is the most stable (RFC 8259, frozen 2017).
#   - It is the substrate's existing first declaration.
#   - Most data-emitting consumers (mcp dispatch, future portal
#     wire serialization per the 2026-06-07 portal-as-eigenvalue-
#     stream insight) pull JSON first.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   json carrier   Crockford (2001), introducing JSON as JavaScript
#                  object-literal interchange. Standardized: ECMA-404
#                  (2013), RFC 7159 (2014), RFC 8259 (2017). The
#                  carrier below is the RFC 8259 §3-5 value grammar.
#   parse/emit     Wadler (1985), "How to replace failure by a list
#                  of successes" — the parser-as-monad form parse
#                  realises (when its body lands). The dual emit
#                  (carrier → text) is the inverse, sometimes called
#                  the pretty-printer (Wadler 2003, "A prettier
#                  printer") — connecting this species directly to
#                  the T17 Wadler/Bernardy cascade tick.
#
# Spec context: docs/research/2026-06-07-pretty-printer-LRM.md
#               (the cascade this species's emit will integrate
#               with at T17 — the Wadler/Bernardy doc combinators
#               become the emit body for structured species),
#               RFC 8259 (the format spec the carrier realises).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (the data-altitude lift).
# Substrate recognitions: [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]],
#                      [[feedback-substrate-pull]],
#                      [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]
#                      (parse/emit bodies are forward-promised;
#                      the boot shard's `\` bodies carry over).
# Related shards: data.mirror (the family root above),
#                 boot/std/data/json.mirror (the floor declaration
#                 this species lifts; retained as transitional
#                 placeholder during consumer migration),
#                 boot/std/mcp.mirror (the consumer still importing
#                 `in @data/json`; migration deferred).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/mirror/data/json.mirror`
# declares `@mirror/data/json` and only that.

# === The json carrier ===
#
# Per RFC 8259 §3-5: six value kinds. Lifted verbatim from
# boot/std/data/json.mirror.
type json = null | bool(bool) | number(f64) | string(text) | array([json]) | object([(text, json)])

# === parse — bytes → json ===
#
# Focus projection at the data altitude: byte sequence in JSON
# wire format yields a typed json carrier. The body is `\` per
# the substrate-pull discipline — lands when the consumer pulls
# (the bootstrap parser today, the @mirror/compile-bootstrapped
# parser when @facet/mirror.render lands at T17).
parse(text) -> json { \ }

# === emit — json → bytes ===
#
# Settle projection at the data altitude: typed json carrier
# yields a byte sequence in JSON wire format. The body is `\` per
# the same discipline; the Wadler/Bernardy combinator cascade at
# T17 lands this body in the substrate (the pretty-printer LRM's
# central recognition: `emit` for structured data IS the same
# operation as `render` for source code).
emit(json) -> text { \ }

out @mirror/data/json
out json
out parse
out emit
